<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Nguyen lords</h1><p class="st-meta">1558 CE – 1777 CE · capital Phu Xuan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southern Vietnamese lords who built Dang Trong, pushed Vietnam into the Mekong delta and founded the line of the last imperial dynasty.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1558 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-hoang">Nguyen Hoang</a>, son of the Le loyalist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-kim">Nguyen Kim</a>, had himself appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuan-hoa">Thuan Hoa</a> in central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a> to escape the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/trinh-lords">Trinh lords</a>. He and his successors built an autonomous realm, Dang Trong, Europeans' <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-cochinchina">Cochinchina</a>, with its capital eventually at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hue">Phu Xuan</a>, present-day Hue.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a> defended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-hoi">Dong Hoi</a> walls against Trinh attacks between 1627 and 1672 with the help of Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a>. They encouraged settlement south into former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> lands, absorbing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panduranga">Panduranga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> delta, and welcomed Chinese Ming loyalists who founded towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-tho">My Tho</a>. Hoi An flourished as an international port.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1771 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tay Son rebellion</a> broke out, and by 1777 the Tay Son had killed most of the Nguyen family. A survivor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Nguyen Anh</a>, fought back from the delta and Siam and in 1802 founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty</a> as emperor Gia Long.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nguyen-lords.jpg" alt="Nguyen lords" width="320" height="533"><figcaption>Image: Own work by uploader · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vietnam1650.GIF" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1558 CE – 1777 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phu Xuan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nguyen Hoang, Nguyen Phuc Nguyen, Nguyen Phuc Khoat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Confucianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vietnamese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dang Trong, Đàng Trong, Cochinchina, Chúa Nguyễn</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1668&amp;focus=nguyen-lords" aria-label="Nguyen lords on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7610 1566 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nguyen lords lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7930 1822L7992 1921M7930 1822L7881 2044M7930 1822L7930 1822M7930 1822L7957 1912"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7992 1921h0M7881 2044h0M7930 1822h0M7957 1912h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7930 1822h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7930 1822h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1668&amp;focus=nguyen-lords">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
